        ":step/content": {
          ":db/id": 17592186088944,
          ":tutorial/content": "A **first-order linear ordinary differential equation** is a differential equation in the variable $y(x)$ that can be expressed in the **general form**\n\n$$\na(x)y'(x)+b(x)y(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $f(x)$ are functions of $x$ only, and\n\n- $a(x)$ is not identically zero (i.e., it is not zero for all $x$).\n\nFor example, the differential equation\n\n$$\nxy' + e^x y=1\n$$\n\nis a first-order linear ODE with $a(x) = x, b(x) = e^x,$ and $f(x) = 1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the differential equation \n\n$$\nx(y')^2 +  e^x \\sin(y)=1\n$$\n\nis *not* a first-order linear ODE. Notice that it contains the terms $(y')^2$ and $\\sin(y),$ and therefore cannot be presented in the general form.",
          ":tutorial/id": "cc4983db-1d7c-5573-a6d1-9e5e4a09207e",
          ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 4055,
          ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
        },
        ":step/id": "6cc3efa3-9929-5b62-9be5-629d74b8499c",
