        }
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186088798,
        ":step/content": {
          ":db/id": 17592186088945,
          ":tutorial/content": "The general form of a first-order linear ODE is\n\n$$\na(x)y'+b(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, if we take the general form and divide both sides by $a(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a(x)}{a(x)}{y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)} \\\\ {y}^{'}+\\frac{b(x)}{a(x)}y & =\\frac{f(x)}{a(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that $a(x)$ is not identically zero, so we're allowed to perform this division.\n\nNow, if we define the functions $P$ and $Q$ as \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{b(x)}{a(x)}, \\qquad Q(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{a(x)},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we can write our equation in the following **standard form**:\n\n$$\ny'+P(x)y =Q(x)\n$$\n\nFor example, given the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\ny' \\sin x+ y e^x= \\cos x,\n$$\n\nthe standard form of this equation can be found by dividing through by $\\sin x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+y\\left(\\frac{{e}^{x}}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\ {y}^{'}+y{e}^{x}\\operatorname{csc}x & =\\operatorname{cot}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nP(x) = e^x \\csc x, \\qquad Q(x) = \\cot x.\n$$",
          ":tutorial/id": "d0517ea0-b60e-5c34-bfbc-41fd6c61af75",
          ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 4056,
          ":tutorial/title": "The Standard Form of a First-Order Linear ODE"
