        ":db/id": 17592186088801,
        ":step/content": {
          ":db/id": 17592186088862,
          ":tutorial/content": "Recall that the standard form of a first-order linear differential equation is\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nNote that $Q(x)$ is called the **forcing function.**\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **homogeneous** if the forcing function $Q(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ In other words, the equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny' + P(x)y = 0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear ODE is **inhomogeneous** if it is not homogeneous.\n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- Suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 0.\n$$\n\nThe equation matches the form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = -x.$ Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Now, consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' - xy = 6.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $Q(x) = 6$ means that the equation cannot be written in the form $y' + P(x)y = 0.$ Therefore, this equation is *not* homogeneous (i.e., it is *inhomogeneous*).\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\ny' + xy = 2y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left-hand side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'}+xy & =2y \\\\ {y}^{'}+xy-2y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{'}+(x-2)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form $y' + P(x)y = 0$ with $P(x) = (x-2).$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.",
          ":tutorial/id": "2107c0cc-b904-547f-b13d-9a0898b33d73",
          ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 14653,
          ":tutorial/title": "Homogeneous and Inhomogeneous Equations"
        },
